# Issue first, then feature branch

Before starting feature work, UI/UX changes, new pages or areas, non-trivial refactors, **or bug fixes**:

1. Create a GitHub issue with `gh issue create` that states the problem and intended outcome.
2. Create and check out a feature branch named for that issue, then do the work on it.

```text
# GOOD
gh issue create --title "TMG header crashes after login refresh" --body "..."
git checkout -b 77-fix-tmg-header-crash-after-refresh

# BAD
Fix a crash on the current feature branch (or main) with no new issue
```

Branch name: `<issue-number>-<short-kebab-title>`.

A crash, regression, or Gea/HMR failure is a **new bug issue**, even if it showed up while working on another issue. Do not fold it into the current branch.

**If unsure** whether this applies (bug vs copy nit, new issue vs existing one, new branch vs stay put), **ask before editing**. Do not guess.

Do not create an issue for one-line typos, copy nits, or work the user already tied to an existing issue or branch. If they name an issue, use that issue and branch from it.
